<MailcowFtsCase
  lang="en"
  title="Full-text search optimization across three Mailcow servers"
  description="A production Dovecot Flatcurve case study: indexing large mail archives, validating shared mailboxes, and measuring server-side search latency."
>
  <p>
    I examined full-text search configuration, Dovecot indexes and body-search behavior
    across three independent Mailcow installations. On one server, an archive of roughly
    1.6 TB included a large INBOX where a message-body search took approximately ten
    minutes in a mail client. The objective was to make search usable without migrating mail.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Implementation</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>FTS diagnosis.</strong> Reviewed Mailcow settings, loaded
        <code>fts</code> and <code>fts_flatcurve</code> plugins, indexer behavior,
        available disk space and process memory limits.</li>
      <li><strong>Archive indexing.</strong> Enabled or verified Flatcurve as needed and
        populated Dovecot indexes sequentially across mailboxes and folders. Initial
        indexing of the server with approximately 1.6 TB of mail took about 6–7 hours.</li>
      <li><strong>Shared mailboxes.</strong> Checked searches from a delegate's shared
        namespace, whose index can differ from the mailbox owner's index. The shared
        INBOX needed its own indexing pass.</li>
      <li><strong>Operational checks.</strong> Ran controlled body-search queries,
        reviewed logs and resource usage, and documented reusable Bash scripts for
        status checks, sequential indexing and benchmarking.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Measured outcomes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Approximately 1.6 TB mail archive.</strong> Before the work, a search
        in the mail client took around <strong>10 minutes</strong>. After indexing,
        <code>doveadm search</code> found <strong>77 messages in 0.519 s</strong>
        within the owner's INBOX.</li>
      <li><strong>Shared INBOX.</strong> Once the delegate's shared index was
        populated, the same <strong>77 messages</strong> were found in
        <strong>0.311 s</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Approximately 71 GiB mailbox on another server.</strong> Three
        post-indexing queries took <strong>2–5 s</strong>. No pre-indexing latency
        baseline was captured for this mailbox.</li>
      <li><strong>Third installation.</strong> FTS checks and indexing work were
        performed; no comparable before/after benchmark was recorded.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Benchmark caveat:</strong> the original ~10-minute figure was observed
      in a mail client. The 0.519 s and 0.311 s figures are server-side
      <code>doveadm</code> command timings, including invocation via Docker Compose.
      Client rendering, network latency and cache warm-up were not independently
      measured. These values should not be presented as a controlled end-to-end
      speedup ratio.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Outcome and references</h2>
    <p>
      Full-text search became practical on large mail archives: controlled server-side
      queries completed in fractions of a second, and another server returned
      post-indexing results in a few seconds. The troubleshooting and indexing
      procedure is documented for future maintenance.
    </p>
